IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `whs-worker`
IMPORT `whs-seat-pcbu`

-- Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Cth) -- the Schedule 4 penalties as indexed under cl 4 and rounded under cl 5, beside
-- whs-worker.l4 and whs-seat-pcbu.l4 (unchanged: their penalty questions keep the printed figures, which are the base
-- here). It calls their penalty rules and restates none of them; the cl 2 and cl 3 tables, which no other module holds,
-- are set out here.
--
-- Formula used (Schedule 4 cl 4(2)), lost from the text deposit in conversion and restored in
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C2011A00137.formulas.md (from the authorised Word rendering of C2024C00243, image2.wmf,
-- checked against the text drawn in the image):
--     A × (B / C)
-- where A is the amount set out in cl 1, 2 or 3; B is the CPI number for the March quarter in the year immediately
-- preceding the year for which the amount is calculated; C is the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022.
-- The CPI numbers are the Australian Statistician's and none is deposited: they are inputs.
--
-- SEAT: the worker, as in whs-worker.l4 ("What is the most I can be fined this year?"), and the person conducting the
-- business or undertaking, as in whs-seat-pcbu.l4. Each question here wraps that seat's own penalty question.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 16 (C2024C00243), as whs-worker.l4. BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Work Health and Safety Act 2011 -- the Schedule 4 penalties as indexed`

§§ `Schedule 4 cls 4, 5, 7 -- how an amount set out is indexed and rounded`

-- cl 7: "year means a period of 12 months starting on 1 July." A year is named here by the calendar year it starts in
-- (2024 for 1 July 2024 to 30 June 2025).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Schedule 4 year that includes` MEANS
    IF DATE_MONTH `the day` AT LEAST 7
    THEN DATE_YEAR `the day`
    ELSE (DATE_YEAR `the day`) MINUS 1

-- cl 5: "If, after indexation under clause 4, the amount of a monetary penalty applying in a year is: (a) less than
-- $10,000 and not a multiple of $100: (i) the amount must be rounded to the nearest $100; and (ii) an amount of $50 is
-- rounded down; or (b) more than $10,000 and not a multiple of $1,000: (i) the amount must be rounded to the nearest
-- $1,000; and (ii) an amount of $500 is rounded down." "An amount of $50" (and of $500) is read as a remainder of exactly
-- half the unit; anything above it goes up, anything below it down. An amount of exactly $10,000 is in neither paragraph,
-- and needs no rounding (NOTES.md finding 20).
GIVEN `the amount` IS A NUMBER
      `the unit` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rounded to the nearest unit, a half unit down` MEANS
    IF `the remainder` EQUALS 0
    THEN `the amount`
    ELSE IF `the remainder` AT MOST (`the unit` DIVIDED BY 2)
         THEN `the amount` MINUS `the remainder`
         ELSE (`the amount` MINUS `the remainder`) PLUS `the unit`
    WHERE
        `the remainder` MEANS `the amount` MINUS ((FLOOR (`the amount` DIVIDED BY `the unit`)) TIMES `the unit`)

GIVEN `the amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rounded under cl 5` MEANS
    IF `the amount` LESS THAN 10000
    THEN `rounded to the nearest unit, a half unit down` `the amount` 100
    ELSE IF `the amount` GREATER THAN 10000
         THEN `rounded to the nearest unit, a half unit down` `the amount` 1000
         ELSE `the amount`

-- cl 4(2): "The amount of a monetary penalty applying in each year is to be calculated as follows: A × (B / C)".
-- cl 4(3): "If the amount of a monetary penalty calculated for a year is less than the amount that applied in the
-- previous year, then the amount for the previous year continues to apply." The amount calculated is compared before
-- rounding, since cl 5 rounds "after indexation under clause 4"; the amount that applied is the one rounded then. Either
-- order gives the same figure, because the earlier amount is already a whole $100 or $1,000 (NOTES.md finding 19).
GIVEN `amount A` IS A NUMBER
      `CPI number B` IS A NUMBER
      `CPI number C` IS A NUMBER
      `the amount that applied in the previous year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount applying in the year` MEANS
    IF `the amount calculated` LESS THAN `the amount that applied in the previous year`
    THEN `the amount that applied in the previous year`
    ELSE `rounded under cl 5` `the amount calculated`
    WHERE
        `the amount calculated` MEANS `amount A` TIMES (`CPI number B` DIVIDED BY `CPI number C`)

-- cl 4(1): "The amount of each monetary penalty set out in clause 1, 2 or 3 must be indexed for the year commencing on
-- 1 July 2024, and for each subsequent year, in accordance with this clause." So each year from 2024 is worked out in
-- turn, each from A (not from the year before) and compared with the year before. The amount that applied in 2023-24 is
-- read as the amount set out (NOTES.md finding 18).
GIVEN `amount A` IS A NUMBER
      `CPI number C` IS A NUMBER
      `the B numbers, a year at a time` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
      `the amount that applied in the previous year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount after each year in turn` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the B numbers, a year at a time`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN `the amount that applied in the previous year`
    WHEN `CPI number B` FOLLOWED BY `the later B numbers`
         THEN `the amount after each year in turn` `amount A` `CPI number C` `the later B numbers` (`the amount applying in the year` `amount A` `CPI number B` `CPI number C` `the amount that applied in the previous year`)

DECLARE `Why no indexed figure` IS ONE OF
    -- cl 4(1); the compilation date is 1 July 2024, and the figures in force before it are not deposited
    `the day is before 1 July 2024: cl 4(1) indexes from the year starting then, and the penalties before this compilation (1 July 2024) are not encoded`
    -- cl 4(2), B: one March-quarter CPI number for each year indexed
    `a CPI number is missing: one is needed for the March quarter of each year from 2024 to the March quarter before the year asked about`

DECLARE `Indexed Schedule 4 amount` IS ONE OF
    `the indexed amount` HAS `for the year starting on 1 July of` IS A NUMBER, `the amount in dollars, indexed and rounded` IS A NUMBER
    `no indexed amount`  HAS `why there is no indexed amount` IS A `Why no indexed figure`

-- B for 2024-25 is the March quarter 2024 (the March quarter in 1 July 2023 to 30 June 2024, the year immediately
-- preceding), for 2025-26 the March quarter 2025, and so on: the year asked needs the numbers from March 2024 to the March
-- quarter of the calendar year it starts in. Numbers beyond those are not used.
GIVEN `amount A` IS A NUMBER
      `the day` IS A DATE
      `CPI number C` IS A NUMBER
      `the B numbers from March 2024` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH AN `Indexed Schedule 4 amount`
`the Schedule 4 amount as indexed` MEANS
    IF `the year` LESS THAN 2024
    THEN `no indexed amount` `the day is before 1 July 2024: cl 4(1) indexes from the year starting then, and the penalties before this compilation (1 July 2024) are not encoded`
    ELSE IF (count `the B numbers from March 2024`) LESS THAN `the years indexed`
         THEN `no indexed amount` `a CPI number is missing: one is needed for the March quarter of each year from 2024 to the March quarter before the year asked about`
         ELSE `the indexed amount` `the year` (`the amount after each year in turn` `amount A` `CPI number C` (take `the years indexed` `the B numbers from March 2024`) `amount A`)
    WHERE
        `the year` MEANS `the Schedule 4 year that includes` `the day`
        `the years indexed` MEANS `the year` MINUS 2023

§§ `ss 28, 30A to 34, Schedule 4 cls 1, 4, 5 -- the most I can be fined, as indexed`

-- The fine set out in Schedule 4 cl 1 is indexed; the years of imprisonment are not (cl 4 indexes "the amount of each
-- monetary penalty"). s 30A's penalties, "25 years imprisonment" and "$18,000,000", are stated in s 30A itself, not in
-- Schedule 4, so cl 4 does not index them (NOTES.md finding 17).
DECLARE `Indexed maximum penalty` IS ONE OF
    `the indexed maximum` HAS `the year starting on 1 July of` IS A NUMBER, `fine in dollars, indexed and rounded` IS A NUMBER, `years of imprisonment, which indexation does not change` IS A NUMBER
    `the s 30A maximum, which Schedule 4 does not index` HAS `fine in dollars, as s 30A states it` IS A NUMBER, `years of imprisonment, as s 30A states it` IS A NUMBER
    `no such offence for this offender`
    `no indexed maximum` HAS `why there is no indexed maximum` IS A `Why no indexed figure`

GIVEN `the printed maximum` IS A MAYBE `Maximum penalty`
      `the offence` IS AN `Offence`
      `the day` IS A DATE
      `CPI number C` IS A NUMBER
      `the B numbers from March 2024` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH AN `Indexed maximum penalty`
`the maximum penalty as indexed` MEANS
    IF (`the Schedule 4 year that includes` `the day`) LESS THAN 2024
    THEN `no indexed maximum` `the day is before 1 July 2024: cl 4(1) indexes from the year starting then, and the penalties before this compilation (1 July 2024) are not encoded`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the printed maximum`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN `no such offence for this offender`
         WHEN JUST `printed` THEN
             IF `the offence` EQUALS `industrial manslaughter: intentional conduct breaching a health and safety duty that causes a death, reckless or negligent as to it (s 30A)`
             THEN `the s 30A maximum, which Schedule 4 does not index` (`printed`'s `fine in dollars, before indexation`) (`printed`'s `years of imprisonment`)
             ELSE CONSIDER `the Schedule 4 amount as indexed` (`printed`'s `fine in dollars, before indexation`) `the day` `CPI number C` `the B numbers from March 2024`
                  WHEN `the indexed amount` `year` `dollars` THEN `the indexed maximum` `year` `dollars` (`printed`'s `years of imprisonment`)
                  WHEN `no indexed amount` `why` THEN `no indexed maximum` `why`

-- The worker's question in whs-worker.l4 (column 3 of cl 1), with its fine indexed.
@export What is the most I can be fined, or imprisoned for, if I breach my duty as a worker, with the fine indexed for the year that includes a given day (ss 28, 30A to 34, Schedule 4 cls 1, 4, 5)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc Which offence: category 1 (negligent or reckless conduct exposing someone to a risk of death or serious injury), category 2 (a failure that exposes someone to that risk), category 3 (any failure), or industrial manslaughter
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc A day in the year (1 July to 30 June) whose penalty amounts you want
      `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` IS A NUMBER @desc The All Groups Consumer Price Index number (weighted average of the 8 capital cities) published by the Australian Statistician for the March quarter of 2022
      `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024` IS A LIST OF NUMBER @desc The same index's number for the March quarter of 2024, then 2025, and so on in order, up to the March quarter of the calendar year in which the year asked about starts (one number for 1 July 2024 to 30 June 2025, two for the year after)
GIVETH AN `Indexed maximum penalty`
`What is the most I can be fined as a worker, as indexed` MEANS
    `the maximum penalty as indexed` (`What is the most I can be fined as a worker` `the offence`) `the offence` `the day` `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024`

-- The PCBU's question in whs-seat-pcbu.l4 (columns 2 and 4 of cl 1), with its fine indexed.
@export What is the most my business, or I as its owner or officer, can be fined or imprisoned for a breach of a health and safety duty, with the fine indexed for the year that includes a given day (ss 30A to 33, Schedule 4 cls 1, 4, 5)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc Which offence: industrial manslaughter, category 1 (negligent or reckless conduct exposing someone to a risk of death or serious injury), category 2 (a failure that exposes someone to that risk) or category 3 (any failure)
      `the business is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender is a body corporate (a company or other corporation) rather than you as an individual conducting the business, or as its officer
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc A day in the year (1 July to 30 June) whose penalty amounts you want
      `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` IS A NUMBER @desc The All Groups Consumer Price Index number (weighted average of the 8 capital cities) published by the Australian Statistician for the March quarter of 2022
      `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024` IS A LIST OF NUMBER @desc The same index's number for the March quarter of 2024, then 2025, and so on in order, up to the March quarter of the calendar year in which the year asked about starts (one number for 1 July 2024 to 30 June 2025, two for the year after)
GIVETH AN `Indexed maximum penalty`
`What is the most the business can be fined, as indexed` MEANS
    `the maximum penalty as indexed` (`What is the most the business can be fined` `the offence` `the business is a body corporate`) `the offence` `the day` `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024`

§§ `Schedule 4 cls 2, 3, 4, 5 -- tier penalties and civil penalties, as indexed`

-- cl 2: "the tier A monetary penalty" to "the tier I monetary penalty", column 2 "An individual", column 3 "A body
-- corporate": A $139,000 / $695,000; B $70,000 / $350,000; C $28,000 / $140,000; D $14,000 / $70,000; E $8,400 / $42,000;
-- F $7,000 / $35,000; G $5,000 / $25,000; H $2,800 / $14,000; I $1,700 / $8,500. The provisions name their tier (s 38(1)
-- and s 39(1) tier D, s 38(7) tier F, s 47(1) tier C, for example).
DECLARE `Tier A to I` IS ONE OF
    `the tier A monetary penalty`
    `the tier B monetary penalty`
    `the tier C monetary penalty`
    `the tier D monetary penalty`
    `the tier E monetary penalty`
    `the tier F monetary penalty`
    `the tier G monetary penalty`
    `the tier H monetary penalty`
    `the tier I monetary penalty`

-- cl 3: "the WHS civil penalty provision tier 1" to "tier 4", column 2 "An individual", column 3 "A body corporate":
-- tier 1 $28,000 / $140,000; tier 2 $14,000 / $70,000; tier 3 $7,000 / $35,000; tier 4 $2,800 / $14,000.
DECLARE `Civil penalty tier` IS ONE OF
    `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 1`
    `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 2`
    `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 3`
    `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 4`

GIVEN `for an individual` IS A NUMBER
      `for a body corporate` IS A NUMBER
      `the offender is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the column for the offender` MEANS
    IF `the offender is a body corporate` THEN `for a body corporate` ELSE `for an individual`

GIVEN `the tier` IS A `Tier A to I`
      `the offender is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount set out in cl 2` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the tier`
    WHEN `the tier A monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 139000 695000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier B monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 70000 350000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier C monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 28000 140000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier D monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 14000 70000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier E monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 8400 42000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier F monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 7000 35000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier G monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 5000 25000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier H monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 2800 14000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the tier I monetary penalty` THEN `the column for the offender` 1700 8500 `the offender is a body corporate`

GIVEN `the tier` IS A `Civil penalty tier`
      `the offender is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount set out in cl 3` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the tier`
    WHEN `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 1` THEN `the column for the offender` 28000 140000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 2` THEN `the column for the offender` 14000 70000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 3` THEN `the column for the offender` 7000 35000 `the offender is a body corporate`
    WHEN `the WHS civil penalty provision tier 4` THEN `the column for the offender` 2800 14000 `the offender is a body corporate`

@export What is the most I can be fined for an offence whose penalty is a tier A to I monetary penalty, indexed for the year that includes a given day (Schedule 4 cls 2, 4, 5)?
GIVEN `the tier` IS A `Tier A to I` @desc The tier the offence's penalty names, for example tier D for not ensuring Comcare is notified of a notifiable incident (s 38(1))
      `I am a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender is a body corporate (a company or other corporation) rather than an individual
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc A day in the year (1 July to 30 June) whose penalty amounts you want
      `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` IS A NUMBER @desc The All Groups Consumer Price Index number (weighted average of the 8 capital cities) published by the Australian Statistician for the March quarter of 2022
      `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024` IS A LIST OF NUMBER @desc The same index's number for the March quarter of 2024, then 2025, and so on in order, up to the March quarter of the calendar year in which the year asked about starts (one number for 1 July 2024 to 30 June 2025, two for the year after)
GIVETH AN `Indexed Schedule 4 amount`
`What is the most I can be fined at a penalty tier, as indexed` MEANS
    `the Schedule 4 amount as indexed` (`the amount set out in cl 2` `the tier` `I am a body corporate`) `the day` `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024`

@export What is the most I can be ordered to pay for contravening a WHS civil penalty provision of tier 1 to 4, indexed for the year that includes a given day (Schedule 4 cls 3, 4, 5)?
GIVEN `the tier` IS A `Civil penalty tier` @desc The tier the WHS civil penalty provision names
      `I am a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who contravened it is a body corporate (a company or other corporation) rather than an individual
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc A day in the year (1 July to 30 June) whose penalty amounts you want
      `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` IS A NUMBER @desc The All Groups Consumer Price Index number (weighted average of the 8 capital cities) published by the Australian Statistician for the March quarter of 2022
      `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024` IS A LIST OF NUMBER @desc The same index's number for the March quarter of 2024, then 2025, and so on in order, up to the March quarter of the calendar year in which the year asked about starts (one number for 1 July 2024 to 30 June 2025, two for the year after)
GIVETH AN `Indexed Schedule 4 amount`
`What is the most I can be ordered to pay at a civil penalty tier, as indexed` MEANS
    `the Schedule 4 amount as indexed` (`the amount set out in cl 3` `the tier` `I am a body corporate`) `the day` `the CPI number for the March quarter of 2022` `the CPI numbers for each March quarter from 2024`
